WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.387
What belongs in the file.

2
00:00:01.667 --> 00:00:10.861
The best-practices doc gives a two-question test before you write anything down: could the agent find this by reading the code, and would it actually go and look?

3
00:00:11.141 --> 00:00:13.381
If both answers are yes, leave it out.

4
00:00:13.661 --> 00:00:22.557
Current models read repositories well; they discover your layout, conventions, test framework, and component patterns faster than you’d explain them.

5
00:00:22.837 --> 00:00:26.485
What they can’t find is information that was never in the repo.

6
00:00:26.765 --> 00:00:34.168
Things that pass the test: How to confirm work: the exact test, typecheck, and lint commands, and how to run one test file.

7
00:00:34.448 --> 00:00:37.883
The docs call the check command “the single most useful line.

8
00:00:38.163 --> 00:00:38.460
”.

9
00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:46.079
Places where the code misleads: an old pattern that’s everywhere but mustn’t be copied, a directory that looks dead but isn’t.

10
00:00:46.359 --> 00:00:53.186
Decisions made outside the code: which error helper the team chose, how deploys work, what needs migration review.

11
00:00:53.466 --> 00:00:55.215
Anything you’ve corrected twice.

12
00:00:55.495 --> 00:01:06.055
The memory docs list the triggers: the same mistake a second time, a review comment the agent should have known, the same correction typed again, context a new teammate would need.

13
00:01:06.335 --> 00:01:18.218
Things that fail it: directory tours, dependency lists, architecture overviews the code already shows, generic advice (“write clean code”), and personal preferences (those go in CLAUDE.

14
00:01:18.498 --> 00:01:19.287
local.

15
00:01:19.567 --> 00:01:21.252
md or your user file).

16
00:01:21.532 --> 00:01:29.703
Things that belong somewhere else: multi-step procedures (a release, an incident runbook) go in a skill, which loads only when needed.

17
00:01:29.983 --> 00:01:33.567
Rules for one part of the codebase go in a path-scoped rule.

18
00:01:33.847 --> 00:01:37.260
Rules with zero exceptions go in a hook or deny rule.

19
00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:42.490
The features overview has a useful trigger table for this, covered in lesson 5.

20
00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:51.218
The target size is under 200 lines per file; longer files cost context on every request and the docs say they reduce adherence.

21
00:01:51.498 --> 00:02:01.738
Simon Willison adds a practical note for tooling: a few examples of how to run your project’s scripts in the instruction file is usually enough for an agent to adapt them.
